WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.000
There's no point in denying that Harrison&nbsp;
Ford has long been considered the resident&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:07.440
grump of the "Star Wars" ensemble. He's&nbsp;
known to be a bit grouchy towards the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:11.600
franchise that gave him his first big break&nbsp;
into movie stardom. He's even gone so far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:15.840
as to repeatedly mention how he wished for&nbsp;
Han Solo to get killed off at some point.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:19.040
Apparently, he simply doesn't like&nbsp;
playing Han Solo and would prefer to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:22.720
take on more three-dimensional characters in&nbsp;
the vein of Rick Deckard from "Blade Runner."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:25.760
But unless you've been vigilant in&nbsp;
reading every scrap of information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:29.200
you can about Ford and his co-stars on the&nbsp;
sets of the original "Star Wars" movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:32.320
you might not be familiar with a&nbsp;
tidbit dropped by one Mark Hamill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:36.800
during a 2015 interview about his memories&nbsp;
of working with Harrison Ford on the films.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:41.120
Before we go on, it's probably a good idea to&nbsp;
point out that Hamill's feelings towards Ford seem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:46.400
to be uniformly, and mutually, positive. In an&nbsp;
interview with Jake's Takes from February of 2020,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:50.880
Ford returned the favor by saying something&nbsp;
quite nice about his Jedi comrade-in-arms:

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:54.000
"When I met Mark Hamill, I really&nbsp;
learned how to be cool ... He's just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.120
a really straightforward nice&nbsp;
guy. He's really a sweet guy."

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:59.200
But Hamill has shared at least one "Star Wars"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:02.480
memory that shows Ford could&nbsp;
be somewhat volatile on set.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:07.120
In a 2015 interview with Empire, Mark Hamill&nbsp;
shared a funny story about an incident involving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:11.360
Harrison Ford and Han Solo's iconic spaceship:&nbsp;
"You heard about Harrison taking a saw to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:15.440
Millennium Falcon because he got so mad? People&nbsp;
were coming up to me, going, 'You gotta stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:19.840
Harrison, he's sawing up the Falcon.' It was&nbsp;
made of wood, and he just took a saw to it."

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:23.120
Hamill didn't share exactly what Ford&nbsp;
was mad about when he decided to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:25.600
such unorthodox use of his skills as a carpenter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:29.040
but he did share that he was able to&nbsp;
bring things to a peaceful resolution.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:32.560
"I love Harrison. I got to stop him because&nbsp;
I can make him laugh when he gets really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:36.880
really mad. And I had no stake in it. He was with&nbsp;
Carrie, and I was still hanging out with Yoda."

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:40.480
The Yoda reference is a clue that the incident&nbsp;
in question happened during filming of "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:43.760
Empire Strikes Back." Fortunately,&nbsp;
Hamill was there to calm Ford down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:47.280
and production of what many fans consider&nbsp;
to be the masterpiece of the entire "Star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:50.720
Wars" saga proceeded to its eventual&nbsp;
creative and commercial success.

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:55.520
Given stories like the one Hamill shared, it'd&nbsp;
be easy to assume Ford is a grump all day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:59.360
every day. However, considering his career&nbsp;
has spanned several decades at this point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:04.240
across numerous different franchises, one has to&nbsp;
assume people enjoy working with him to an extent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:06.400
After all, no matter how good of an actor you are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:10.880
a bad attitude is a surefire way to sink&nbsp;
any career, no matter what your name is.

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:14.080
Fortunately, it appears Ford knows&nbsp;
when to cut loose and have fun.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:16.640
In the same Empire interview,&nbsp;
Hamill goes on to tell a story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:20.400
of how Ford knows when to be silly,&nbsp;
as well. Hamill goes on to relate,

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:23.600
"He's got a wonderfully wacky sense&nbsp;
of [humor]. I'll give you an example.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:28.320
This disco song that goes, 'More, more, more, how&nbsp;
d'ya like it, how d'ya like it,' remember that?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:32.240
We used to love really bad pop songs, so&nbsp;
whenever that song would come on I'd run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:35.760
and turn it on. And he'd come into&nbsp;
my dressing room and start dancing."

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:38.720
You aren't alone if you're having a&nbsp;
difficult time envisioning Harrison&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:43.280
Ford dancing along to disco songs. As it&nbsp;
turns out, there's a good reason for that:

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:46.320
"The minute somebody comes to the door,&nbsp;
he'd stop dead and stand there. 'Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:49.440
okay, I'll be down to the set in a&nbsp;
minute.' As soon as the door closes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:52.480
it's, 'Get the action going!'&nbsp;
He'd only do it around me."

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:54.960
It sounds as though Ford and&nbsp;
Hamill have a special bond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:58.560
and there's a specific image Ford&nbsp;
wants emitted to the general public.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:03.920
Still, we'd give anything to see that&nbsp;
footage of Ford dancing along to Andrea True.

